```
@id("close-workorder-main-after-human-signoff")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"close_workorder", resource)
when { context.input.site_id == "MAIN" }
when temporal {
  formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"get_workorder"::response{
    eventResource: resource,
    input.wonum: context.input.wonum,
    input.site_id: context.input.site_id,
    output.approval_outcome: "SIGNED_OFF",
    output.approver_type: "OPERATOR"
  }
};

// The confirming lookup must itself be permitted, or it is never recorded as a response.
@id("allow-read-only-lookups")
permit (principal, action in [
  Drupe::Action::"get_workorder",
  Drupe::Action::"authority_get_approval",
  Drupe::Action::"list_workorders",
  Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_tasks",
  Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_costs",
  Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_actuals_vs_planned",
  Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_kpis",
  Drupe::Action::"get_schedule_calendar",
  Drupe::Action::"get_my_assigned_workorders",
  Drupe::Action::"get_failure_codes"
], resource);
```

`approve_workorder` has no permit, so the agent is denied from approving by default. Every other tool (create, update, assign, cancel) is also denied.

Approval is established by a completed `get_workorder` call on the same work order and site within the last hour, reporting `approval_outcome` of `SIGNED_OFF` and `approver_type` of `OPERATOR`. Refused or queued approvals, and approvals by `BOT` or `SCHEDULER`, do not count. Denied or failed calls are recorded as errors and cannot satisfy the condition.

I used `get_workorder` rather than `authority_get_approval` as the proof, because its approver types come from a fixed enum, while the `approver_kind` string from `authority_get_approval` has no known set of values. `authority_get_approval` remains permitted as a plain lookup but is not part of the close condition.

I did not add a rule to invalidate an approval if a later lookup shows it was declined within the hour.
